WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.467 --> 00:00:04.924
RoboRecast: Intent-Preserving Demonstration Augmentation

2
00:00:05.067 --> 00:00:08.257
via Diagnosis-Driven Optimization.

3
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.713
We begin with the results of our real-world experiments.

4
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:18.665
The first task is to place a cube in a bowl.

5
00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:24.666
With 50 real demonstrations, the policy succeeds in 20% of trials.

6
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.183
In the centre, we reconstruct the original demonstration.

7
00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:34.059
On the right, we change where the cube and bowl start.

8
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:39.134
Now compare the two policies on the real robot.

9
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:47.763
Adding reconstructed and augmented data raises success from 20% to 55%.

10
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:53.222
These training examples can also come from people.

11
00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:58.696
Below each demonstration, a simulated arm carries out the same task.

12
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.634
Watch how it follows the hand to open the microwave.

13
00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:07.766
How do the trained policies perform on the real robot?

14
00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:13.496
Across these four trials, the arm stacks, places, pushes and opens.

15
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.421
Next, we change what the robot handles.

16
00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:24.831
In the top row, it handles a mouse, a controller and a mug.

17
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:29.491
In the bottom row, the tasks carry over to a different arm.

18
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:36.070
We trained these policies entirely on the synthetic data just shown.

19
00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:39.306
Here they are running on real robots.

20
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.089
The top row shows adaptation to new objects.

21
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.513
The bottom row shows adaptation to a different robot arm.

22
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:55.388
Next, we reconstruct and augment several kinds of real-world demonstrations.

23
00:01:57.133 --> 00:02:00.887
The original arm places a tomato in a steel bowl.

24
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.706
On the right, both the arm and the layout have changed.

25
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.904
The new arm lowers a banana onto a blue plate.

26
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:14.685
These examples from DROID involve articulated objects.

27
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.867
The drawer slides, the doors swing on hinges, and the tap spout rotates.

28
00:02:20.267 --> 00:02:24.929
Each reconstruction follows the motion in the real demonstration.

29
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.230
With two arms, the movements have to stay coordinated.

30
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:35.892
On the right, a different pair of arms works around a silver microwave.

31
00:02:36.433 --> 00:02:40.286
The arms now place the green cup instead of the blue bowl.

32
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.476
The source can even be a pair of human hands.

33
00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:51.368
Follow the bowls on the left as the person stacks them.

34
00:02:52.733 --> 00:02:56.386
On the right, two robot arms reproduce that sequence.

35
00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.663
For the final example, the robot itself moves through the scene.

36
00:03:03.633 --> 00:03:06.460
Watch the turn as it approaches the chair.

37
00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:11.417
Both grippers hold the backrest as the robot pushes the chair in.

